    },
    {
      "text": "If you were just to say yes, you know, instead of like beautiful Waymos that we have driving us around in San Francisco, you'd have something like this, you know.",
      "timestamp_sec": 134,
      "why": "The horses-with-rockets analogy that anchors his argument against pure customer deference."
    },
    {
      "text": "But you actually want to deliver good software. You need to build the right thing. So, you don't just endlessly say yes to people.",
      "timestamp_sec": 175,
      "why": "Compresses the scoping principle into its operative form."
    },
    {
      "text": "So, it's Friday night and an enterprise sales rep comes to us with an urgent request that this super important strategic logo is only going to close if we build out an SAP S/4HANA integration.",
      "timestamp_sec": 175,
      "why": "The concrete scenario the entire first half is reasoned against."
    },
    {
      "text": "I've seen sales reps who like go kind of crazy because it's like the end of the quarter and they're trying to hit their quota and close the deal and not because the customer is the one driving the urgency.",
      "timestamp_sec": 228,
      "why": "Names a specific organizational incentive distortion FDEs must filter for."
    },
    {
      "text": "But, I'd say the most important thing that an FDE does is also looks beyond this one request and looks at the other prospects that are coming down the pipeline and other customers to see if anyone else would benefit from this as well.",
      "timestamp_sec": 264,
      "why": "Identifies what he considers the single highest-value scoping behavior."
    },
    {
      "text": "And that's when they told us they only they they they require they mandate all of their employees to use iOS devices.",
      "timestamp_sec": 307,
      "why": "The punchline of the failure story that justifies front-loaded scoping."
    },
    {
      "text": "Even some of the most basic assumptions like which you know mobile platform you build on it's it's super important um to validate them",
      "timestamp_sec": 351,
      "why": "The generalized lesson extracted from the Android incident."
    },
    {
      "text": "So, unless you are scaling with model capabilities, you are going to fall behind. Now, I'm not going to belabor this point too much.",
      "timestamp_sec": 388,
      "why": "States the second principle as a competitive necessity."
    },
    {
      "text": "the point is that we basically have to reinvent our jobs constantly now. So, whatever work we are doing today, you know, for the most part it's knowledge work, we have to figure out how to have models and agents do it for us.",
      "timestamp_sec": 388,
      "why": "Frames the automation mandate as continuous self-replacement of knowledge work."
    },
    {
      "text": "From gathering context to scoping out a request to writing out a spec and then implementing the feature, each stage of that pipeline can be replaced with agents.",
      "timestamp_sec": 429,
      "why": "Defines the decomposition that makes the automation problem tractable."
    },
    {
      "text": "the latency of replies went from like hours or days to like, you know, seconds",
      "timestamp_sec": 550,
      "why": "Reports the concrete effect of the V1 intake agent."
    },
    {
      "text": "I I would say it's probably saved us like a large percentage, I don't know, 20% of the time that we'd spend on scoping out these requests.",
      "timestamp_sec": 590,
      "why": "The talk's only quantified outcome, with its uncertainty stated."
    },
    {
      "text": "The last step as well, going from a a well-shaped spec to like a working product, obviously like Frontier models can like one-shot medium-size features.",
      "timestamp_sec": 628,
      "why": "A claim about current model capability that others might contest."
    },
    {
      "text": "It's this middle part that I would say is super like gnarly and like unformed and difficult.",
      "timestamp_sec": 628,
      "why": "Locates where the real unsolved work sits in the pipeline."
    },
    {
      "text": "Imagine like all the knowledge that a product manager has in their head about their product. Like, how do you get that into an agent?",
      "timestamp_sec": 715,
      "why": "Frames tacit context transfer as the core unsolved engineering problem."
    },
    {
      "text": "ultimately the most important thing here is that as an FD, we still have the responsibility of taste and judgment over the final output.",
      "timestamp_sec": 715,
      "why": "Marks the boundary he draws around what stays human."
    },
    {
      "text": "If you don't do a good job of scoping out requests or or building upon the principles of scoping things well, you're going to get a token maxing slop cannon.",
      "timestamp_sec": 757,
      "why": "The memorable formulation of why automation without scoping backfires."
    },
    {
      "text": "If you are, you know, amazing at scoping, but don't invest in building out this, you know, agent factory, you know, it's going to be over for you.",
      "timestamp_sec": 757,
      "why": "Completes the symmetry argument that both principles are mandatory."
    },
    {
      "text": "Always be scoping and scaling with tokens. The future of FD needs both.",
      "timestamp_sec": 799,
      "why": "The closing thesis in one line."
    }
